To determine how many functions are both **even** and **odd**, we begin by recalling the definitions of these two types of functions.

---

### Definition Recap

- An **even** function satisfies the condition:  
  $$
  f(-x) = f(x) \quad \text{for all } x \text{ in the domain}
  $$

- An **odd** function satisfies the condition:  
  $$
  f(-x) = -f(x) \quad \text{for all } x \text{ in the domain}
  $$

---

### Combining Both Conditions

If a function is **both even and odd**, it must satisfy both of the above conditions simultaneously. So:

$$
f(-x) = f(x) \quad \text{(from evenness)}
$$
$$
f(-x) = -f(x) \quad \text{(from oddness)}
$$

Equating the two expressions for $ f(-x) $:

$$
f(x) = -f(x)
$$

Adding $ f(x) $ to both sides:

$$
2f(x) = 0
$$

Dividing both sides by 2:

$$
f(x) = 0
$$

---

### Conclusion

The only function that satisfies both even and odd properties is the **zero function**:

$$
f(x) = 0 \quad \text{for all } x \in \mathbb{R}
$$

This is the **only** such function, as any deviation from zero would violate either the even or odd condition.

---

